{"step_index":601,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:57:15Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-07T07:57:15+01:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-07T07:57:15+01:00\nShowing lines 250-267 of 267\n250: ## 12. High-Definition Screenshot Engine (`tools/screenshot_engine.py`)\n251: \n252: ### 12.1 Purpose & Sandbox Immunity\n253: Automated browser screenshot utilities (Puppeteer, Playwright, Selenium, Headless Chrome) frequently fail inside restricted Windows sandbox environments due to rendering pipe and process isolation limits.\n254: `tools/screenshot_engine.py` circumvents this limitation entirely by utilizing Pillow (`PIL`) to programmatically render 1600x1000 pixel high-definition visual representations of all 10 dashboard states:\n255: - Employs dark cyber SOC color palettes (`(8, 12, 20)` deep obsidian background, `(6, 182, 212)` neon cyan accents, `(239, 68, 68)` critical red badges, `(16, 185, 129)` emerald safe indicators).\n256: - Draws HUD headers, active view tabs, live KPI metrics cards, and structured tables with telemetry badges.\n257: - Exports production-ready PNGs directly into `screenshots/`.\n258: \n259: ---\n260: \n261: ## 13. Persistent Synchronization Policy\n262: \n263: In accordance with the \"Update & Append Always\" directive:\n264: - Every execution cycle preserves agent state and session artifacts into `brain_archive/`.\n265: - All architectural updates, CLI commands, and database structures are maintained across `docs/` and synced upstream to GitHub.\n266: \n267: "}
